{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1490 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "77e6934eb6341cb45ae96a837e2203c19bfdfe91ed3b76a3238d6d9ecb3a651c" :edb/db-before-t 1489 :edb/db-after-t 1490 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534802 50 #inst "2026-10-12T15:11:44.456Z" 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186192784 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436607 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186192784 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436610 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186192785 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436609 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186192785 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436611 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186192790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436604 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186192790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436608 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186192790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436612 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186192790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436615 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186192794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436605 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186192794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436606 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186192794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436613 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186192794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436614 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186192923 1050 960.0 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186193890 1064 "We can use the chain, product, and quotient rules to find partial derivatives of multivariable functions. \n\nFor example, consider the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\cos(xy^2) + 3x\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}y}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}y}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $x,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x{y}^{2})+3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+3\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}x}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}x}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $y,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}(x{y}^{2})+3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x{y}^{2})\\cdot x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})+0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x{y}^{2})\\cdot x\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =-2xy\\operatorname{sin}(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534802 17592186045417 false] [17592186193890 1064 "We can use the chain, product, and quotient rules to find partial derivatives of multivariable functions. \n\nFor example, consider the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\cos(xy^2) + 3x\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}y}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}y}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $x,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2})+3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+3\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}x}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}x}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $y,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2})+3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})+0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot x\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =-2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186436532 1080 "12" 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186436532 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186436532 1083 #uuid "07492d45-331a-54bb-8e47-f1b2b223890f" 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186436533 1080 "{y}^{2}(2x+{x}^{3}){e}^{{x}^{2}}" 13194139534802 17592186045417 true] [17592186436533 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534802 1759218604541